            },
            "createdOn": 1764754810,
            "author": {
                "name": "saipavann27",
                "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                "username": "saipavann27"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "What DBUS property uses ERROR (in all caps)?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "saipavann27",
                        "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                        "username": "saipavann27"
                    },
                    "message": "Before: \"ERROR\" was treated as a valid probe token, causing false positives.\nAfter: \"ERROR\" is no longer matched; only defined tokens are recognized. No standard D-Bus property uses \"ERROR\"\u2014it\u2019s a message type, so treating it as a probe token was incorrect."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "You misunderstand, I'm not aware of any interface that uses ERROR in its probe string.  Is that something you've added?\n\nThis patch is fine on its own, I just don't understand how this bug was found."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "saipavann27",
                        "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                        "username": "saipavann27"
                    },
                    "message": "I didn\u2019t add ERROR anywhere \u2014 the bug was found during testing on our board.\nReference(https://github.com/openbmc/entity-manager/issues/24)\n\n1. How I discovered the issue:\nWhile probing xyz.openbmc_project.UsbDevice({'VENDOR_ID': '2frrr3'}), the probe was incorrectly treated as an OR conjunction because vendOR_id contains the substring \"OR\".\n\n2. How I confirmed the logic problem:\nI tried other probe strings like:\n\nvendOR_id \u2192 contains \"OR\" \u2192 returns probe_type_codes::OR.\n\nTRUEcolor=blue \u2192 contains \"TRUE\" \u2192 returns probe_type_codes::TRUE_T.\n\ndeviceMATCH_ONEvalue \u2192 returns probe_type_codes::MATCH_ONE.\n\nThis showed that the current logic (probe.find(...)) matches substrings, not whole words.\n\n3. How I fixed it:\nUpdated the code to use regex with word boundaries\n\n4. How I tested:\n\nxyz.openbmc_project.UsbDevice({'VENDOR_ID': '2frrr3'}) \u2192 now returns nullopt (correct).\nproperty='vendOR_id' \u2192 no match (correct).\n\nThis ensures only standalone tokens like OR, AND, TRUE are matched, fixing the false positives."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "saipavann27",
                        "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                        "username": "saipavann27"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Would be nice to have a unit test for it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "regex can be slow sometimes, I assume it'll be OK here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "saipavann27",
                        "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                        "username": "saipavann27"
                    },
                    "message": "@ed@tanous.net, @vernon.mauery@gmail.com could you please review the PR.Thanks"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "saipavann27",
                        "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                        "username": "saipavann27"
                    },
                    "message": "@ed@tanous.net,\nCould you please check this,\n\nI didn\u2019t add ERROR anywhere \u2014 the bug was found during testing on our board.\nReference(https://github.com/openbmc/entity-manager/issues/24)\n\nHow I discovered the issue:\nWhile probing xyz.openbmc_project.UsbDevice({'VENDOR_ID': '2frrr3'}), the probe was incorrectly treated as an OR conjunction because vendOR_id contains the substring \"OR\".\n\nHow I confirmed the logic problem:\nI tried other probe strings like:\n\nvendOR_id \u2192 contains \"OR\" \u2192 returns probe_type_codes::OR.\n\nTRUEcolor=blue \u2192 contains \"TRUE\" \u2192 returns probe_type_codes::TRUE_T.\n\ndeviceMATCH_ONEvalue \u2192 returns probe_type_codes::MATCH_ONE.\n\nThis showed that the current logic (probe.find(...)) matches substrings, not whole words.\n\nHow I fixed it:\nUpdated the code to use regex with word boundaries\n\nHow I tested:\n\nxyz.openbmc_project.UsbDevice({'VENDOR_ID': '2frrr3'}) \u2192 now returns nullopt (correct).\nproperty='vendOR_id' \u2192 no match (correct).\n\nThis ensures only standalone tokens like OR, AND, TRUE are matched, fixing the false positives"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/entity_manager/perform_probe.cpp",
                    "line": 235,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm not a huge fan of using regex here.  std::regex is insanely slow, we're making a string copy to use it;  We don't really expect anything other than space here, so I suspect we can just check for that?\n\nThis fix also looks incorrect for things that aren't conjunctions.  \"FALSE\" is a complete statement, and doesn't require a word boundary.\n\nLong term, I would like to see a real parser integrated, like:\nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/36517 so that we're operating on an AST and tokens, not string parsing.\n\nShort term, would simply changing the regex match for \"AND\" and \"OR\" to \" AND \" and \" OR \" fix this without needing regex, while still keeping the unary functions working?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/entity_manager/perform_probe.cpp",
                    "line": 235,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "saipavann27",
                        "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                        "username": "saipavann27"
                    },
                    "message": "To fix this kind of issues added the logic:\nFor instance: probe = xyz.openbmc_project.UsbDevice({'VENDOR_ID': '2frrr3'}) would fail to be probed and would be considered as a conjunction statement - since it has OR at 36th index (in vendOR_id)\nNote: please Suggest the any testcases want to test."
                }
            ],
